>
	<span slot="title">Trade memory: what is stored and what is retrieved</span>
	<p slot="subtitle" class="subtitle">An LLM without history repeats its own mistakes with fresh confidence. This article describes the storage, the retrieval rules, and where the design is still thin.</p>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>Why there is memory at all</h2>
		<p>The first version of the engine (December 2025) was a stateless prompt pipeline: every cycle computed indicators, formatted them and asked the model for a decision. A position opened in one cycle was unknown to the next one, because nothing recorded why it had been opened. The first fix was a local JSON state file; it was fragile and lost data when the process stopped mid-write.</p>
		<p>State fixed continuity but not learning. Two files handle that now: SQLite (<code>trade_history.db</code>) is the record of what happened, and ChromaDB is what can be searched by similarity. The rest of this article is about the second one.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What is stored</h2>
		<p>Closed-trade experiences are embedded locally with <code>BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5</code> — <strong>768 dimensions</strong>, no embedding API call, no trade data leaving the machine. Three ChromaDB collections hold the memory:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><code>trading_experiences</code> — closed trades: the market context, the decision, the outcome and the P&amp;L;</li>
			<li><code>semantic_rules</code> — rules the reflection pass has written from results, in plain language;</li>
			<li><code>system_constraints_rejections</code> — signals that were refused, with the reason.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Confidence statistics are not a fourth collection. They are computed on demand from the same embeddings (<code>compute_confidence_stats</code>, <code>src/trading/vector_memory.py</code>), so there is one source of truth for "how is this going?" and it cannot disagree with the stored trades.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What is retrieved before a decision</h2>
		<p>The brain assembles a bounded context for every cycle (<code>src/trading/brain.py</code>):</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>3 most similar past trades</strong> — over-fetched, then re-scored 70% similarity / 30% recency, each with a match breakdown;</li>
			<li><strong>win rate and average P&amp;L over the 20 nearest trades</strong> in the current market context;</li>
			<li><strong>3 learned rules</strong> that match the current regime (trend, ADX, volatility label);</li>
			<li><strong>up to 5 recent post-mortems</strong> written by the model after closed trades.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>That is the whole memory block — nothing else is injected, and the prompt budget silently drops whole lower-priority sections when the context would overflow (<code>[prompt_budget]</code>). When the sample is too thin, the prompt says so in capitals: <em>"⚠️ LIMITED DATA: treat these as ANECDOTES, not as an established pattern"</em>. With a dozen closed trades, most retrievals are in that state, and it would be dishonest to present them otherwise.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>The surprise ratio</h2>
		<p>Not every outcome is equally informative. A trade that won because of a random headline teaches the wrong lesson about the setup. Each closed trade therefore gets a surprise score:</p>
		<div class="formula-box">
			Surprise Ratio = |Realized P&amp;L − Expected P&amp;L| / |Expected P&amp;L|
		</div>
		<p>Anything above <strong>1.5</strong> is tagged <code>⚠️ high surprise</code> in vector memory, and the retrieval tells the model to discount that experience. The expected P&amp;L in the denominator comes from the bot's own estimate, so the score inherits any error in that estimate — it measures the gap between plan and result, not an independent truth.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>Rules, not just examples</h2>
		<p>After every <em>N</em> closed trades a reflection pass (<code>src/trading/brain.py</code>, <code>src/trading/post_mortem.py</code>) reviews outcomes and writes rules in plain language, tagged by type: <code>anti_pattern</code> (things to avoid), <code>corrective</code> (adjustments to the current approach) and best practice. Rules carry an evidence count and a decay score (<code>src/trading/vector_memory_rules.py</code>) and are retrieved by similarity to the current conditions, not by recency. A matched anti-pattern forces the model to state the conflict and lower its confidence instead of passing over it.</p>
		<p>The learned thresholds are bounded: the brain can raise the entry R/R floor, never lower it below the configured <code>min_rr_entry</code> (<code>src/trading/rr_policy.py</code>). A model that writes its own rules should not be able to talk itself into worse risk.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What this does not prove</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>The sample is small.</strong> The ledger window has 12 closed trades. Retrieval over 12 vectors is closer to anecdote lookup than to a track record, and the prompt labels it that way.</li>
			<li><strong>Similarity is not equivalence.</strong> Two setups that are close in embedding space are close in the text used to describe them; that does not make them the same market situation.</li>
			<li><strong>Rules are advice, not code.</strong> The model can read a learned rule and ignore it. Only guards and the R/R policy are enforced.</li>
			<li><strong>No controlled comparison.</strong> There is no experiment here showing that memory improves results; the memory exists because the stateless version could not explain its own decisions.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

